2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {}
With the 4×5 corner the white group D1–D2 has the single liberty D3.  On the
bounded board D1 has no right–hand or down neighbour and D2 has no right–hand
neighbour, so after the position is shown

* W D1–D2 : {D3}
* B C1–C2 : {B1,B2}
* B A2 : {A1,A3,B2}
* B B3–B4 : {A3,A4,B2}

the only legal way to avoid an immediate self–capture is to play D3.  D3
merges the D1–D2 eye with C3–C4 and gives the merged white group the two
real liberties D4 and C5, both of which are self–atari for Black.

After 1.W D3 Black can always keep the three black groups alive, e.g.

* 1…B B1   gives the C1–C2 group the liberties A1,B2;
* 1…B B2   joins A2–B3–B4–C1–C2 into one group with liberties A1,A3,A4,B1.

In either case the mutual atari on B1/B2 is resolved in Black’s favour and
White has no move that reduces any black group to zero liberties, nor a
second move that is forced after the D3 saving move.  In particular no first
white move can capture a black group at once and the only saving move D3
does not create a forced kill.  Hence there is no first move that initiates a
winning kill sequence.

1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A1, B1}

I think White has two kill-initiating moves here. B1 is the direct tesuji: it ataris the C1-C2 stones, forcing Black to connect at B2, after which White plays A1 to remove the corner liberty. Black’s group then has only A3/A4 as liberties, and White can chase with A3 or A5 to capture.

A1 also seems to initiate the same kill. If Black answers B1, White cuts at B2, capturing the C1-C2-B1 stones. The remaining A2/B3/B4 group cannot make two eyes: Black must try to run with A3/A4, but White replies at A4/A3 respectively, and the group runs out of liberties. So I would list the possible killing moves as {A1, B1}.

