3.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board is treated locally, corner A1, with no edge forced on D1-D2, so White is not forced to play D3 first. The three Black complexes remain

* Ga = A2  L = {A1,A3,B2}
* Gb = B3–B4 L = {A3,A4,B2}
* Gc = C1–C2 L = {B1,B2}

B2 is the unique joining point of the three complexes and B1 is the unique atari point of Gc. Occupying any of the five liberty points {A1,A3,A4,B1,B2} removes a liberty from ≥2 complexes and puts the White stone played into a mutual atari.

* 1.W B1 2.B B2 forced else Gc falls. After 2.B B2 the merged A2-B2-B3-B4-C1-C2 has L={A1,A3,A4} and W B1 is in atari on A1. 3.W A3 / 3.W A4 → 4.B A1 captures B1, leaving L={A4,B1} / L={A3,B1} and 5.W A4 / 5.W A3 finishes the net.
* 1.W B2 makes L(Ga)={A1,A3}, L(Gb)={A3,A4}, L(Gc)={B1}. 2.B B1 forced else W B1 captures Gc. After 2.B B1 GcB1 has L={A1} and Ga/Gb is {A3,A4}-netted; 3.W A1 captures GcB1 and the A3/A4 chase finishes A2-B3-B4.

The same A1-A3-A4/B1 miai net is obtained symmetrically from the other three liberty points. 1.W A1 → 2.B B2 merges to L={A3,A4,B1}; 1.W A3 → Black’s best is B2/B1 and leaves the merged mass with at most {A1,B1,A4}; 1.W A4 is symmetric to A3. In each case after the forced Black reply B1 or B2 the remaining Black material is reduced to the {A1,A4,B1} miai and White can fill the last liberties with A3/A4.

Current round consensus is moving toward the full five-point set {A1,A3,A4,B1,B2}. The B1-only and B2-only claims are sufficient but not exclusive; transposition shows A1, A3, A4 also initiate the same forced A1-A5/B1-B2 net. The {} claim rests on the erroneous edge assumption that D1-D2 is in atari on D3 and that 1.W D3 is forced; on a 19×19/local corner D1-D2 has liberties E1/E2/D3 and the right-hand White group is safe, so the attack on the left net is available. The outlier {D3} is not a killing initiation. Remaining nuance is the exact capture order after A1, but the mutual-atari net is forced and no Black defence makes two eyes.

12.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The D1-D2 atari is a forced sacrifice. Any first move ≠ D3 lets Black play D3 and capture D1-D2, but the capture does not save the three black complexes A2, B3-B4 and C1-C2; it only adds D1/D2 as extra liberties to C1-C2 and is still compatible with the net. The core shape is

Gc = C1-C2 L={B1,B2}
Ga = A2 L={A1,A3,B2}
Gb = B3-B4 L={A3,A4,B2}

Play on any of the five shared liberties removes a liberty from at least two of the complexes and creates a mutual atari that forces Black to merge. The classic cutting atari is B2:

1.W B2 makes L(Gc)={B1}, L(Ga)={A1,A3}, L(Gb)={A3,A4}. Black must answer B1 or lose Gc immediately. 1…B B1 makes B1-C1-C2 1-liberty on A1. 2.W A1 captures B1-C1-C2 and the remainder Ga/Gb is A3/A4 netted, e.g. 4.B A3 5.W A4. The same net transposes from the symmetric atari 1.W B1 → 2.B B2. After 1.W B1 the merged A2-B2-B3-B4-C1-C2 has L={A1,A3,A4} with the white B1 stone in atari on A1; 3.W A3/A4 4.B A1 and 5.W A4/A3 finishes the mass.

A1, A3 and A4 are the other liberty points of the three-complex net. 1.W A1 makes Ga 2-liberty {A3,B2}. Black’s natural 1…B B1 captures A1 but leaves GcB1 with L={A1,B2} and Ga/Gb still tied on B2/A3/A4. 2.W B2 then forces the A1 capture of GcB1 and an A3/A4 chase on Ga/Gb. 1…B B2 after A1 merges all three complexes to L={A3,A4,B1} and the same A3/A4 miai finishes. 1.W A3 makes Ga 2-liberty {A1,B2} and Gb 2-liberty {A4,B2} and any Black reply B2/A1/A4/B1 leaves at most the three liberties A1,B1,A4 to be taken in miai. 1.W A4 is symmetric to A3 on Gb.

Thus up to the D1-D2 sacrifice the five points A1,A3,A4,B1,B2 all initiate a forced A1-A5/B1-B2 net. The disagreement in the room is about whether the white atari D1-D2 must be saved. If White is required to keep D1-D2 alive the only non-losing first move is 1.W D3 and after 1.W D3 Black can keep the three complexes alive, e.g. 1…B B2 → L={A1,A3,A4,B1}, and the right-hand attack on C5/D4 is available. Under the usual tesuji convention that a sacrifice is allowed the first-move kill initiators are the full liberty set {A1,A3,A4,B1,B2}, with B2 and B1 being the most commonly quoted minimal moves. The empty-set / D3-only view corresponds to the stricter requirement that White’s own group must survive.

